# Fixed shooter, 1981 -- evocations, not the originals.
#
# The arcade used a Namco WSG for the short effects and sampled waveforms for the longer
# ones, so as with the maze game nothing here can be a copy. What is worth reproducing is
# the shape of the set: this game's sounds are unusually long for 1981, and several of them
# are pitch gestures that last a whole second or more while something moves on screen.
#
# That is the thing to take from it. Most arcade sound is a fiftieth of a second; a capture
# beam holding for a second and a half is what makes an event feel like an event.

bank   Starfighter
clock  1512000
tick   220

category Weapons

sound Player fire
audf  20 1 +9 14
audc  170 1 -1 10
amp   0.4
note  Short, high, falling. A fixed shooter fires constantly, so the shot has to be brief
note  enough to overlap itself without turning to mud.

sound Enemy fire
audf  70 1 +6 12
audc  73 1 0 12
amp   0.3
note  Lower and rougher than yours, which is the entire design rule for enemy weapons.

category Impacts

sound Enemy hit
audf  25 2 +13 18
audc  140 2 -1 13
amp   0.45
note  Noise sweeping down while it fades. The workhorse -- it plays more than anything
note  else in the game, so it is kept short and mid-loud.

sound Player hit
audf  35 3 +8 26
audc  141 3 -1 14
amp   0.6
note  The same idea, three times as long and much louder. Nothing else in the bank runs
note  this long at this volume, which is what makes it read as yours.

category States

sound Capture beam
audf  200 3 -6 30
audc  40 3 0 30
amp   0.4
note  Well over a second of slow rise on the 5-bit poly. The signature sound of the game
note  and the reason its set is remembered -- almost nothing else from 1981 holds a single
note  gesture this long.

sound Rescue
audf  120 2 -5 24
audc  170 2 0 20
audc  170 2 -1 6
amp   0.45
note  The beam's answer: shorter, cleaner, pure tone instead of poly. Same direction, so
note  the pair reads as question and reply.

category Movement

sound Dive
audf  50 1 +5 26
audf  180 1 -6 22
audc  40 1 0 48
amp   0.35
note  Down then up, one continuous gesture -- the shape of something swooping past and
note  pulling away. Two AUDF steps in a row is all it takes.

sound Formation
audf  110 2 -4 14
audc  170 2 0 14
amp   0.3
note  A short rise played as each rank arrives. Quiet on purpose: it repeats many times a
note  wave, and anything louder would exhaust its welcome by level three.

category Pickups

sound Extra life
audf  80 1 0 4
audf  60 1 0 4
audf  40 1 0 10
audc  170 1 0 18
amp   0.45
note  Three flat rising notes. An arpeggio says reward in a way a sweep never does.

category Interface

sound Blip
audf  45 1 0 3
audc  164 1 0 3
amp   0.3
note  Fourteen milliseconds. A menu sound that outstays that becomes hateful by the tenth
note  press.
